WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.960
Justice League didn't hold back on any of
the big moments.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:06.810
We got a planet-threatening disaster, a team
of superheroes determined to stop it, and

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.750
a good old-fashioned race between Superman
and Flash.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.480
Now that justice has been served on the big
screen, we're doing justice to the little

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.029
moments and small details you may have missed
in Justice League.

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:19.099
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:19.099 --> 00:00:20.099
Crisis cameo

00:00:20.099 --> 00:00:23.529
Early in the film, you may have caught that
newspaper headline from the Metropolis Post

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:26.570
asking if a bunch of fallen heroes returned
to their own planets.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.980
What you may not have noticed was the smaller
headline beside the main story—"Citywide

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.309
Crisis."

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:36.829
In the DC universe, a crisis is kind of a
big deal, and DC doesn't throw the C-word

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:38.370
around casually.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:43.360
It started in Justice League of America #21
with the Crisis on Earth One story line.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.449
In the very next issue, there was Crisis on
Earth Two.

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:51.960
Later, 1985's game-changing Crisis on Infinite
Earths story arc put the entire multiverse

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.129
in peril.

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:58.671
Various crises even popped up after that,
like Infinite Crisis, Identity Crisis, and

00:00:58.671 --> 00:01:00.249
Final Crisis.

00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:04.780
Basically, most of DC comics' history has
been defined by crises, so name-dropping the

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.420
word in a DCEU movie is definitely a piece
of fan service.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.700
Smooth finish

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.840
For the majority of the movie, Cyborg had
a notably different look than he does in the

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:14.909
comics.

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:18.890
Most versions of Cyborg in the comics have
a smooth, aerodynamic exterior.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:23.540
In Justice League, however, Cyborg had sharp
edges sticking out everywhere, like a sentient

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.119
pile of cutlery.

00:01:25.119 --> 00:01:29.130
But at the end of Justice League, Vic uses
his growing control over the Mother Box tech

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:33.979
to give himself a makeover, ditching the RoboCop
2 vibe for a polished shell lifted straight

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:35.030
from the comics.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.420
And while we're on the topic of Cyborg…

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.000
Booyah

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:43.899
Throughout Cyborg's run on Teen Titans and
Teen Titans Go!, Sparky has had one distinguishing

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:44.899
catchphrase:

00:01:44.899 --> 00:01:45.950
"Just practicing my Booyahs."

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.829
Boo Boo Boo Ya Ya Ya :Booyah."

00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:53.270
It takes most of Justice League to get there,
but right at the end he drops his big line,

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.259
giving at least some of the audience a reason
to get out of their seats with a smile.

00:01:57.259 --> 00:01:58.259
"Booyah"

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:01.640
If you've never come across Cyborg in any
form before Justice League, you'd never know

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.890
what all the fuss was about.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.280
"Booyah."

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.680
Mera

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.369
Amber Heard's character, Mera, didn't get
a ton of screen time in Justice League, but

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:14.290
the costume designers definitely paid homage
to Mera's comics wardrobe.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:18.240
From her crown to her green-scaled armor,
the likeness is exact.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.930
Unfortunately, Justice League left the fate
of Mera—and the nature of her relationship

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:26.680
with Aquaman—up in the air, but since Amber
Heard is slated to star in the upcoming Aquaman

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.849
film, it looks like we'll get to see what
finally happens with the future queen of Atlantis.

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:33.400
Red sky of doom

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.230
Leading up to the climax of Justice League,
Steppenwolf takes the Mother Boxes back to

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:42.269
that little Russian town to start his Apokalips
terraforming picnic, and the sky turns blood

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:43.269
red.

00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:47.790
On the surface, that red sky is pure Apokalips—Steppenwolf's
turning Earth into a version of his reddish

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.250
world, after all.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.959
But under the surface, that red sky represents
very DC-specific levels of doom.

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:59.050
Leading up to and during the Crisis on Infinite
Earths storyline, DC comic artists started

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.959
coloring the skies red in their other comic
books.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:05.849
Most of the time, that was the only tie-in,
which is the reason panels like this showed

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:07.440
up in comics like Swamp Thing.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.810
So while those red skies made sense in the
movie, they also represented DC's classic

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.810
way of saying, "Big things are going down."

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.129
Gorilla sign language

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:21.760
If you could give the Flash an iconic nemesis
that wasn't another speedster, it would undoubtedly

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.420
be Gorilla Grodd, a hyper-intelligent ape
who's got a hyper-personal vendetta against

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:27.560
the Flash.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.290
Obviously, there weren't any giant gorillas
in Justice League, but there was a mention

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:33.900
of that classic villain, albeit briefly.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.849
When Bruce Wayne tracks down Allen, the super-speedster
tries to convince Wayne that he's not really

00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:40.120
the Flash, which leads to this line:

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.250
"Fluent in sign language.

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:43.420
Gorilla sign language."

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:47.230
For fans of the comics, it was immediately
obvious that Allen was referring to Grodd,

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.330
the greatest gorilla in the DC universe.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:52.750
At least, outside of Jackanapes, the Joker's
adopted gorilla son.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.590
But this was much more likely a sly reference
to Grodd.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:57.950
Cold resistance

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:01.780
In that same scene where Bruce Wayne first
meets Barry Allen, there are a ton of things

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.290
going on in the background.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.459
If you weren't paying attention to the Rick
&amp; Morty episode, you might have noticed something

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:08.220
else on one of those screens.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.560
Look closely at the monitor directly behind
and to the left of Ezra Miller, and pay attention

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.840
to the words at the top: Cold resistance.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.440
Clearly, Barry has been testing his suit's
tolerance for a chill, and one particular

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.270
Flash villain comes to mind when you think
of cold—Captain Cold, aka Leonard Snart,

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.850
one of Flash's deadliest villains.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.040
Does this mean that Flash has already defeated
Captain Cold in this universe?

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:37.290
Or was he gearing up for a confrontation when
Steppenwolf, uh, stepped on everyone's plans?

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.140
We just don't know!

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.510
Ace Chemicals

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:44.041
There were a good handful of Easter eggs emblazoned
on the buildings throughout the film, one

00:04:44.041 --> 00:04:46.960
of which was a small neon sign advertising
Ace Chemicals.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.160
And if you know your comics history, you know
where this is going.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.640
Yup, Ace Chemicals is the chemical plant where
the Joker fell into a toxic vat and became,

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.110
well, the Joker.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:08.450
It's gone by different names in different
versions of the origin story—it was called

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:13.580
Axis Chemicals in 1989's Batman and, oddly,
Monarch Playing Card Company in the original

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:18.610
run of Detective Comics—but Ace Chemicals
is now DC canon for the name of the birthplace

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.960
of the Joker.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.740
In Justice League, the sign is a blink-and-you'll-miss-it
detail, but it's a nice touch on the world-building

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:26.220
of the movie.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:27.760
Gotham gargoyle

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.400
Nothing says Batman like standing on a gargoyle
high above Gotham on a dark, stormy night.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.810
If you could put that image in a bottle, it'd
smell like the inside of Batman's cowl, guaranteed.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:42.710
So the shot of Batman on a Grim Reaper gargoyle
in the Justice League Comic-Con trailer was

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.880
a definite crowd pleaser.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.640
If you've done your homework and read all
gazillion issues of Detective Comics, you

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.600
might recognize that Reaper image.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:55.500
The shot is a direct homage to the cover of
Detective Comics #682.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:56.520
Ancient king

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.160
After Steppenwolf's first invasion of Earth,
the three Mother Boxes were split among the

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.160
races.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.990
The Atlanteans and Amazons built cool little
shrines for their boxes, but the Men, being

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:10.470
men, decided that they couldn't trust anyone,
so they buried theirs out in the woods.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:15.240
IMDb only lists the main guy there as the
"Ancient King of Men,", but there's one particular

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:20.350
ancient king of men who definitely plays a
role in the DC universe: King Arthur.

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:24.580
Most of the mythological lore of King Arthur
is kept intact in the DCU, so it's definitely

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:28.020
possible that he was the one who would be
entrusted with the Mother Box after a war

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.060
that happened hundreds of years ago.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.060
Super plaid

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.090
Superman is the worst at disguises.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:37.270
But that's part of who he is, so we're all
okay with that by now.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:41.810
Then, somewhere along the line, live-action
adaptations of Superman decided that he also

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:46.330
needed to add plaid to his repertoire of implausibly
common wardrobe choices.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:50.240
Maybe it's because he grew up on a farm in
Kansas, and that's what farmer's wear?

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.940
Whatever the reason, ol' Clark's been putting
on plaid whenever he visits the family farm

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:56.340
ever since 1978's Superman.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.210
It's usually red, but not always.

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:01.870
It was white in Superman IV ["four"] and white
again in Superman Returns.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:05.680
Man of Steel got nervous and put his mom in
plaid, but, oh, there's Clark wearing plaid

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.690
again in Batman versus Superman.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.370
All the way in Metropolis too.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:14.210
"Some of his shirts have some of the plaid
in them as a little reminder of the midwestern

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:15.600
boy from Kansas."

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.540
And who can forget the CW's Smallville, a
show that spent more on plaid than Sister

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:22.190
Act's entire budget for nun costumes?

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:25.620
Which brings us back to Justice League, where
Clark heads out to the farm in Smallville

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.020
to talk to Lois.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.410
In plaid.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.440
Even though she totally knows who he is, and
he can just wear the suit with the big S.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:36.240
She just called him Clark in front of a dozen
policemen while he was Supermanning the Super

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.240
Friends.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.110
So why did he change to plaid?

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.480
Because it's an Easter egg.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:46.030
Look, the bottom line is: if you ever see
a guy anywhere wearing plaid, there's a 30%

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.710
chance it's Superman.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.000
Thanks for watching!

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.500
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.650
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

